course: dictionary
lesson: 6071
title: Merry and its many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Merry usually means happy, cheerful and full of fun. Context helps you check this. In «the merry home-coming» to «red-curtained windows», the people are glad to be back, so merry must mean joyful and warm, not just loud.
explain: Ask yourself: what word would fit here? In "Merry Christmas!", swap in "happy" and try it: "Happy Christmas!" still makes sense, so merry means happy in this greeting. Trying your own word in the sentence is a quick check.
explain: Merry can also be used informally in the UK to mean a little drunk, but that is an adult meaning. Clues in the sentence show which meaning is being used. If a sentence is about a party, a song or a greeting, it almost always means happy and full of fun.
fact: Merry means happy, cheerful and full of fun.
fact: «Merry Christmas!» is a greeting that wishes someone a happy time.
fact: Merry has comparative forms: merrier and merriest, as in «the more the merrier».
fact: «Make merry» means to have fun and celebrate, and a merry-go-round is a ride that goes round and round.
fact: In the UK, informal use of merry can mean a little drunk, as in «merry» and «squiffy». This is an adult meaning, so check the context.
exercise: «The children gave a merry shout as they ran into the garden.» What does merry mean here? | *Happy and full of fun | Tired and sleepy | Angry and loud | Hungry
exercise: We wished Grandma a merry Christmas. What does merry mean in this greeting? | Long and slow | *A cheerful wish for a happy time | Sad and lonely | Very cold
exercise: Which word is a synonym for merry? | sad | cross | tired | *cheerful
exercise: Jayden says merry means quiet. Why is he wrong? | Merry means silent | Merry means cold | Merry means a kind of tree | *Merry means happy, cheerful and full of fun
test: Which word is the opposite of merry? | Cheerful | *Gloomy | Full of fun | Happy
test: «Do you know any merry songs?» Which word would you use to say the happiest song of all? | merrier | merryish | merry | *merriest
exam: Which meaning fits best in «the merry home-coming» to «red-curtained windows»? | Angry and noisy | Cold and wet | Quiet and empty | *Happy and warm
